Recorded worlds
===============

A recording is every packet a server sent from configuration onwards, as it
arrived. Played back, the same handlers see the same packets, so a picture of
a recording is the same picture every time, whatever the server does next.

The recordings themselves are not in the repository: they are server output,
made locally. Only this file is.

Naming
------

One recording per block, named after its area and what it shows:

    <area>-<what>.plrc

    world-nether.plrc      entities-lobby.plrc     data-inventory.plrc
    world-end.plrc         sound-anvil.plrc

The areas are the ones in docs/CONVENTIONS.md: world, entities, player, ui,
sound, data. Recordings are append-only: a recording that is wrong gets a new
name, it is never overwritten, so a test that used it keeps meaning what it
meant. spawn-200.plrc and glass.plrc predate this rule and keep their names.

Making one
----------

    plasma-client record <host>[:port] corpus/<area>-<what>.plrc [chunks] [name]

It records until that many chunks (200 by default) have arrived and two
seconds more, so the light and entities that follow them are in it too. The
name is the player it joins as, PlasmaShot by default; give it your own when
another recording may be running.

The benchmark's four worlds
---------------------------

worldbench needs a square of chunks big enough that a render distance means
something: at distance d a vanilla client holds (2d+1) by (2d+1) chunks, so 289
at 8, 1089 at 16 and 4225 at 32. A server only sends what its view-distance
covers, so the square is recorded by standing in four places that overlap:

    plasma-client record 127.0.0.1:25601 corpus/world-village.plrc 1200 PlasmaB4 &
    for dx in -64 64; do for dz in -64 64; do
        echo "tp PlasmaB4 $((X + dx)) 200 $((Z + dz))" > ~/plasma-testserver/console.in
        sleep 60   # longer the first time: the server is generating the terrain
    done; done

Four stops 64 blocks apart cover 33 by 33 chunks at view-distance 12, which is
render distance 16. A bigger square needs either a bigger view-distance on the
server or more stops; raise view-distance in server.properties if you do, and
put it back. Spectator mode keeps the recorder from falling or being hurt while
it waits.

The four spots are the four scenes docs/METHOD.md asks for, found with the
server's own /locate: a plains village (1456, -368), a dark forest
(1536, -1696), open ocean (1408, -1344) and jagged peaks (1216, -1888) in the
test server's world. Another world has them somewhere else; /locate finds them.

Using one
---------

worldshot, hudshot and menushot take replay:<file> in place of a server:

    plasma-client worldshot replay:corpus/spawn-200.plrc out.png 329

A new command that reads a world should do the same; SessionOptions::replay
is all it takes.
